<p>Preparing for the PyData Berlin talk on <strong>risk budget optimization for causal mix models</strong>. The core idea:</p>
<ul>
<li>You have multiple measurement models (MMM, geo-experiments, attribution) that each estimate channel effects — and they disagree.</li>
<li>Instead of picking a winner, treat the model disagreement as <em>uncertainty</em> and optimize budgets under a risk constraint (e.g., CVaR on the expected return).</li>
</ul>
<p>Open questions I’m chewing on:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>How to elicit a coherent prior across models that disagree on <em>structure</em>, not just magnitude?</li>
<li>Is CVaR the right risk measure here, or does a Bayesian expected-shortfall read more naturally to a marketing stakeholder?</li>
<li>How much does the adstock/saturation functional form bias the risk-optimal allocation vs.&nbsp;the mean-optimal one?</li>
</ol>
<p>The talk draft is in the <a href="articles.html">articles</a> as <em>Bayesian Models and Risk Optimization</em>. Slides coming together.</p>


